2026-09-24T21:01:26.847979Z [] {'source_reference_id': 'DFAT-1112', 'source_id': 'austender', 'title': 'To establish Contract for Design of Investing in education program', 'source_url': 'https://www.tenders.gov.au/Atm/Show/abfde26a-615b-44e6-a1da-7891176dd23d', 'issuing_agency': 'Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade - Australian Aid Program', 'category': 'Request for Tender', 'status': 'open', 'publish_date': '2026-08-28', 'closing_date': '2026-09-25', 'value_amount': 495000.0, 'value_currency': 'AUD', 'value_notes': 'Upper limit, inclusive of GST', 'location': 'Cambodia', 'description': 'The Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) is seeking a supplier to design a new education program in Cambodia, provisionally named the "Investing in Education Program." The program itself has an indicative timeframe of ten years (July 2027 to June 2037) with an initial five-year phase (2027-2032) and a budget of up to AUD 50 million, with AUD 25 million allocated for the first five years. The design contract, DFAT-1112, has an upper limit of AUD$495,000 inclusive of GST.\n\nThe scope of work involves producing an Investment Design Document (IDD) that assesses and recommends appropriate delivery and implementation arrangements, considering value-for-money, feasibility, and sustainability. The design will focus on improving learning outcomes, participation, and completion at the lower secondary level, aligning with Cambodian government priorities and Australia\'s development objectives. Key areas include teacher workforce development, curriculum reform, inclusive education, Gender Equality, Disability Inclusion, and Social Inclusion (GEDSI), and climate change resilience. Deliverables include a Design Plan, Options Paper, Aide M?moire, and multiple drafts of the IDD, along with supporting documents like a draft Statement of Requirements and Risk Register.\n\nThe Request for Tender (RFT) was released on 28 August 2026, and responses close on 25 September 2026 at 10:00 am (ACT Local Time). The timeframe for delivering the design is six months (October 2026 to March 2027), with an option for a three-month extension. An in-country design mission is expected in early December 2026, with the final IDD due in March 2027.\n\nNotably, organisations awarded the design contract are eligible to bid for the subsequent five-year program implementation contract. DFAT has limited flexibility on design timelines but will consider justified alternative schedules. Tenderers are encouraged to propose additional research, include Cambodian nationals or regional experts in their design team, and facilitate local capacity development through junior staff participation.', 'summary_headline': 'DFAT seeks supplier to design the Investing in Education Program for Cambodia.', 'contact_name': 'Tender Secretariat', 'contact_email': 'Tender.Cambodia@dfat.gov.au', 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': 'www.tenders.gov.au', 'documents': [{'file_name': 'Addendum 1 - DFAT- 1112 Design of Investing in education program.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \np.1 of 1 \n  \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nADDENDUM 1 to the Request for Tender (RFT) for DFAT-1112 Design of Investing in education program released \non 28 August 2026.  \n \n \nResponses to follow-up questions submitted to the mailbox: \nAll other information as set out in the RFT dated 28 August 2026 remains unchanged. \n--- End of Addendum 1 --- \n \nQuestion \nAnswer \n1 \nIf a tenderer is awarded the contract to \ndeliver the design of the Cambodia \nInvesting in Education Program is it \npossible for them to also tender and be \nawarded the contract to implement the \ndownstream 5-year program? \n \nYes, organisations can participate in the design or \nevaluation of a DFAT project and then bid for subsequent \nwork for that same project. Further detail is available \nhere: Practice note: guidance on managing perceived \nadvantage in procurement for foreign assistance projects \n| Australian Government Department of Foreign Affairs \nand Trade \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Addendum 2 -- DFAT- 1112 Design of Investing in education program.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \np.1 of 1 \n  \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nADDENDUM 2 to the Request for Tender (RFT) for DFAT-1112 Design of Investing in education program released \non 28 August 2026.  \n \n \nResponses to follow-up questions submitted to the mailbox: \nAll other information as set out in the RFT dated 28 August 2026 remains unchanged. \n--- End of Addendum 2 --- \n \nQuestion \nAnswer \n1 \nCould DFAT please provide the indicative \nbudget range, expected contract value, \nupper funding limit, or anticipated budget \nfor this procurement to assist suppliers in \ndeveloping proposals that are \nproportionate to the required scope, \nmethodology, fieldwork and deliverables? \n \nThe upper limit of this procurement is AUD$495,000 \ninclusive of GST.  \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Addendum 3 - DFAT- 1112 Design of Investing in education program.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \np.1 of 3 \n  \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nADDENDUM 3 to the Request for Tender (RFT) for DFAT-1112 Design of Investing in education program released \non 28 August 2026.  \n \n \nResponses to follow-up questions submitted to the mailbox: \n \nQuestion \nAnswer \n1 \nDoes DFAT have flexibility regarding the \ntimelines of the design process? \n   \nDFAT has limited flexibility regarding the design process \ntimelines however DFAT will consider proposals with \nalternative timeframes where these are clearly justified \nand remain feasible within the design requirements.  \nTenderers should propose a realistic schedule and clearly \nidentify any timing assumptions or constraints that may \naffect delivery.  \n \n2 \nWhat is the distinction between the \ninformation required in the tables under \nSections 5.1 and 5.2? Should tenderers \nprovide different information for each? \n \nPlease refer to drafting notes under Section 5.1 and 5.2. \n \nSection 5.1 should outline the maximum fixed price of \nthe contract including all expenses. \n \nSection 5.2 should propose a progressive payment \nschedule for the Fixed Fees and Charges portion of the \nmaximum fixed price. \n \n3 \nDoes DFAT require a detailed breakdown \nof the pricing suggested in Section 5.1? \nTenderers should provide sufficient breakdown to allow \nDFAT to make a value for money assessment. \n4 \nInvestment funding envelope: Section 1 \nof the Terms of Reference describes an \ninvestment of up to AUD50 million over \nten years, but with no specific program \nbudget for the five year IDD. Could DFAT \nconfirm that the AUD50 million indicative \ninvestment envelope covers the full ten \nyears, July 2027?June 2037, and advise \nwhether an indicative allocation has been \nestablished for the initial five-year \nphase? \nDFAT confirms that the investment is valued up to AUD50 \nmillion over the ten years (2027-2037). The indicative \nallocation for the first five years is AUD$25 million.  \n5 \nMission dates and deliverable timetable: \nCould DFAT confirm the expected dates \nand duration of the in-country mission \nand reconcile the deliverable dates? The \nTerms of Reference envisage an early \nDecember mission with the Options \nPaper presented at its conclusion, while \nATM clause A.A.2(e) specifies deadlines \nof 1 December for the Options Paper, 14 \nDecember for the Aide M?moire and 31 \nDecember 2026 for the first draft IDD. \nDFAT confirms that the expected in country mission date \nin the Terms of Reference is indicative. The in-country \ndesign mission is expected to take place in early \nDecember. Final milestone dates will be confirmed in the \ncontract.  \n\n \n \np.2 of 3 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nPlease also confirm the intended DFAT \nreview periods. \n \n6 \nDual in-country engagements: Noting \nthat the Terms of Reference allow \nadditional visits subject to time and \nfunding, would DFAT consider a proposal \nthat includes two planned visits: an initial \ndiagnostic and consultation mission, \nfollowed by a visit to test and refine the \npreferred design with Cambodian \nstakeholders? \n \nDFAT would consider a proposal that includes two \nplanned in-country visits, provided this can be \naccommodated within the available funding envelope.  \nTenderers should clearly justify the purpose, timing and \nvalue of each visit.  \n \n \n7 \nPrimary research requirements: Beyond \nstakeholder consultations, provincial \nengagement and Theory of Change \nworkshops, does DFAT expect primary \nresearch such as surveys, structured \nclassroom observations or formal \nbaseline data collection, or should \ntenderers propose any additional \nresearch they consider necessary? \n \nTenderers should propose any additional research that \nthey consider necessary.  \n8 \nData and reporting: Noting the indicators \nand information systems identified in \nCambodia?s Education Strategic Plan \n2024?2028, are there specific indicators \nor reporting requirements that this \ninvestment must incorporate? Will the \nDesign Team have access to relevant \nschool-level EMIS data, learning \nassessment datasets and unpublished \nstudies, and are there known access \nrestrictions or material data gaps? \n \nSpecific indicators and reporting requirements will be \nagreed during the design process and should be proposed \nby the Design Team based on the investment?s final \nobjectives, theory of change and feasibility of \nmeasurement. DFAT will facilitate access to relevant \navailable data, including EMIS, learning assessment data \nand relevant studies, where possible.  \nTenderers should identify any anticipated data gaps or \naccess constraints, and propose appropriate mitigation \nmeasures. \n \n9 \nDMEL Standards document: Could DFAT \nprovide an accessible copy of the \nFebruary 2026 Design, Monitoring, \nEvaluation and Learning Standards \nreferenced in the ATM, together with any \nrequired design and reporting templates? \nThe supplied standards link points to a \nDFAT SharePoint location. \n \nThe Design and Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning \nStandards are available on the DFAT Website. \n10 Flexibility in teaching approaches: To \nwhat degree are educational approaches \nare fixed, and how much freedom would \nthe team have to recommend different \n(evidence-based) ways of teaching? \nTenderers may propose evidence-based teaching \napproaches as part of their proposals, where these are \nrelevant and appropriately justified. \n\n \n \np.3 of 3 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nAll other information as set out in the RFT dated 28 August 2026 remains unchanged. \n--- End of Addendum 3 --- \n \nDFAT has undertaken a preliminary Political Economy \nAnalysis and the Design Team is expected to further test \nand refine this analysis through the design process.  \n \n \n11 Where to focus support: what is the \nbalance expected between education \nsystem reform and practical \nimprovements in schools and classrooms \n(curriculum, assessment, teacher \npractice, learning materials)?  \n \nTenderers should use the indicative IOs and EOPOs in the \nTerms of Reference to inform the expected level of \nchange. Given the size of the investment, system-level \nreform may be more appropriate at the higher-level \ngoal/objective, while the design should consider  \npractical improvements in teacher training, schools and \nclassrooms that are most likely to improve learning \noutcomes. \n \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1112 - CCS Approach to Market - InvestIn Education for Cambodia Design.txt', 'extracted_text': "Approach to Market\nTo establish Contract for Design of Investing in education program\nReference ID: DFAT-1112\nUNSPSC: 80100000 Management advisory services\nThis Approach to Market (ATM) is for the provision of: design for the new education program in Cambodia. The design team will produce an Investment Design Document (IDD), for the Education in Cambodia Program for an indicative timeframe of up to ten years (July 2027 to June 2037), with an initial five-year phase (2027-2032) and a budget of up to AUD 50 million.\nThe Commonwealth of Australia as represented by Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (the Customer) is seeking submissions for the provision of the services (the Requirement) as described in this ATM comprising:\nthis CCS ATM, including the Statement of Requirement\nthe CCS ATM Response Form\nthe CCS ATM Annexes (if any)\nthe Commonwealth ATM Terms\nthe Additional Contract Terms (if any)\nthe Commonwealth Contract Terms\nthe Commonwealth Contracting Suite Glossary and Interpretation.\nIn submitting a response, Potential Suppliers are required to comply with all requirements set out in the Commonwealth Approach to Market Terms (a copy of which is included in this document), and if successful, agree to enter into a contract which incorporates the Commonwealth Contract Terms available at https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-contracting-suite-ccs#ccs-terms.\nThe Selling to Government website provides information to help businesses understand how to sell to the Australian Government. If you have any general questions about how the Australian Government procures goods and services, please contact the Selling to Government team at\u202fsellingtogov@finance.gov.au.\xa0\nFor questions relating specifically to this ATM, please contact the ATM Contact Officer set out at A.A.5(a).\xa0\nStatement of Requirement\nA.A.1\tKey Information and Dates\nApproach to Market\nProposed Contract\nA.A.2\tThe Requirement\nThe Design Team will produce an Investment Design Document (IDD) for the proposed InvestIn Education for Cambodia Program for an indicative timeframe of up to ten years (July 2027 to June 2037), with an initial five-year phase (2027-2032) and a budget of up to AUD 50 million. The design will assess and recommend appropriate delivery and implementation arrangements, including consideration of value-for-money, feasibility and sustainably. It will consider implementation approaches that enable future planning, continuation and/or expansion of the investment beyond 2032, subject to mid-term review and performance, and accommodate different funding scenarios above and below the indicative AUD 50 million allocation to ensure flexibility and scalability.\nThe IDD will reflect a consultative design process that tests, confirms, refines and, where appropriate, revises the indicative End of Program Outcomes (EOPO) and Intermediate Outcomes (IOs) outlined in the Investment Concept Note (ICN). The Design Team may recommend alternative outcomes structures where supported by evidence, stakeholder consultation and analysis.\nThe design will reflect and align with the priorities of the RGC, including the Education Strategic Plan 2024-2030 and Vision 2050, and be consistent with Ministry of Education, Youth and Sport (MoEYS) standards for teacher performance, curriculum reform and inclusive education. It will also reflect and align with the directions and priorities of the Australia-Cambodia Development Partnership Plan (DPP), ensuring coherence with Australia?s broader development cooperation objectives in Cambodia. The IDD will include a robust program logic, measurable outcomes, and gender-, disability- and inclusion-sensitive indicators.\nThe indicative EOPOs and IO?s outlined in the ICN are: \nEOPO 1: Teachers in targeted lower secondary schools deliver more effective, inclusive, and evidence-based teaching that improves student engagement and learning outcomes.\nIntermediate Outcomes: \nTeachers at targeted lower secondary schools demonstrate more inclusive and evidence-based teaching practices, including the effective use of formative assessment and differentiated instruction.\nTargeted Teacher Education Colleges provide sustainable systems of continuous professional development that strengthen teaching quality across lower secondary schools in their region.\nEducation authorities use data and evidence to continuously improve teacher professional development and instructional support.\nEOPO 2: Targeted lower secondary schools provide safer, more inclusive, and resilient learning environments that support equitable participation and continuity of learning.\nIntermediate Outcomes:\nSchool leaders and teachers implement gender-responsive pedagogies and positive behaviour approaches that promote equitable participation and foster safe, respectful learning environments.\nTargeted schools strengthen inclusive education practices and provide appropriate support for students with disability and other learners at risk of exclusion.\nTargeted schools and education institutions strengthen disaster preparedness and climate resilience to maintain safe and continuous learning during climate- and disaster-related disruptions\nScope\nThe design will examine the challenges, opportunities and constraints affecting learning outcomes, participation and completion at the lower secondary level in Cambodia. Drawing on stakeholder consultations, analytical work, international evidence, and lesson from existing investments, the design will assess how Australia can most effectively support improved teaching and learning outcomes within the lower secondary sub-sector, while aligning with Cambodia?s education reform priorities and Australia?s broader development objectives. \nThe design will assess the relevance and feasibility of different intervention pathways, including teacher workforce development systems, continuous professional development, curriculum and assessment reform, teaching and learning materials and inclusive education approaches and institutional strengthening at national and sub-national levels. Particular attention should be given to understanding the factors that constrain learning outcomes, participation and transition through lower secondary education, especially for children with disabilities, Indigenous learners, rural students and boys at risk of underperformance. \nThe design will examine the institutional, policy and operating environment within which the investment will be implemented, including MoEYS, Provincial and District Offices of Education, Teacher Education Colleges, schools, development partners and other stakeholders. The design should address opportunities to strengthen the implemention of national reforms at sub-national level and school levels and identify the most effective balance between national, sub-national and school level engagement. The design will also consider recent policy and institutional developments, including the Education Strategic Plan 2024-2028, Teacher Policy Action Plan 2024-2030, Education 2040 Roadmap, Pentagonal Strategy Phase 1, Vision 2050 and relevant climate change inclusion policies. The design should assess how the proposed investments and contribute to Cambodia?s human capital development objectives and complement existing development partner investments. This should include consideration of how evidence-based approaches currently being implemented at primary level, including differentiated learning, structured pedagogy and teacher professional development models, may be adapted or extended to the lower secondary context.\nThe design will examine a range of delivery models and implementation arrangements, including multilateral partnerships, grant mechanisms NGO partnerships, research partnerships, managing contractor approaches, and combinations thereof. The analysis should assess the relative advantages and disadvantages of each option, including feasibility, value-for-money, scalability, sustainability, risk, localisation, implementation complexity and alignment with Australia?s comparative advantage. The Design Team should recommend a preferred approach and provide a clear rationale for that recommendation.\nThe design will identify opportunities to strengthen GEDSI throughout the investment. This should include consideration of barriers faced by children with disability, girls, boys, Indigenous learners and others marginalised groups. The design should examine the factors affecting enrolment, retention, participation and learning outcomes, including any gendered drivers of dropout and barriers affecting children with disabilities.\nThe design will also assess climate change and disaster risks relevant to the education sector and identify opportunities to strengthen resilience and continuity of learning. Particular attention should be given to local and practical and cost-effective approach that can be integrated into the investment and sustained by Cambodia? institutions over time.\nDrawing on consultations, evidence and analysis the Design Team will recommend the most effective approach for Australia?s engagement in the lower secondary sub-sector. This should include recommendations on reform priorities, geographic focus, implementation arrangements, partnership models, governance arrangements, delivery modality and opportunities for phased expansion of the investment over time. Recommendations should be supported by evidence, stakeholder consultation and value-for-money considerations. \nKey Design Questions\nIn undertaking the design, the Design Team should address the following key design questions:\nWhat is the optimal balance between national, sub-national and school-level support required to improve learning outcomes and completion rates?\nWhich intervention pathways offer the strongest evidence of effectiveness and value for money?\nTo what extent can differentiated learning, structured pedagogy and teacher professional development approaches be adapted effectively to lower secondary education?\nWhat factors influence dropout, retention and participation in lower secondary education, including gender-specific drivers?\nWhat barriers prevent children with disabilities from enrolling, participating and succeeding in lower secondary education?\nWhich delivery modality provides the best balance of effectiveness, sustainability, localisation and engagement?\nThe Design Team may propose additional key design questions through the Design Plan.\nDeliverables\nThe Design Team will produce an IDD that meets DFAT?s Investment Design Quality Criteria and Design, Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning (DMEL) Standards. The IDD will include a robust program logic, Monitoring, Evaluation, Research and Learning (MERL) framework, risk assessment and management strategy, governance arrangements, budget, and an implementation approach suitable for procurement and implementation.\nThe Design Team will develop a Design Plan, in consultation with DFAT, that meets DFAT?s DMEL Standards. The Design Team will be responsible for organising stakeholder consultations, with DFAT providing limited support where required.\nThe Design Team will develop an Options Paper (PowerPoint) for DFAT, drawing on information gathered through stakeholder consultations and analytical work from the What Works Hub for Global Education (WWGHE). The Options Paper will be discussed and agreed at the conclusion of the in-country mission. It will summarise:\nkey findings from desk-based research and consultations;\nprovide recommendations on the most effective approach for supporting improved teaching and learning outcomes within Cambodia's lower secondary sub-sector, including reform priorities, geographic focus, delivery pathways and supporting rationale;\noutline options for the national and sub-national allocation of resources;\nprovide recommendations on geographic focus, including specific institutions to be targeted or a methodology for identifying priority locations and institutions; \nrecommend feasible delivery options and explain why the proposed arrangements represent value-for-money; and \nidentify any potential quick wins.\nWithin one week of the completion of the in-country mission, the Design Team will finalise an aide m?moire (PowerPoint presentation and document) reflecting the key findings and agreed outcomes from discussions on the options paper.\nThe Design Team will prepare first and second draft versions of the IDD for DFAT review and input, followed by a final IDD that meets DFAT?s DMEL Standards. The final IDD will include a full description of the rationale for the investment, including justification for investing in Cambodia?s lower secondary education sub-sector. This will include problem analysis and identify Australia?s comparative advantage within the available budget envelope. \nThe IDD will examine the feasibility and viability of available delivery options, including multilateral grant, NGO partnership, managing contractor, or a combination thereof, and explain why the proposed arrangements represent value for money.\nThe IDD will include a program logic with achievable and sustainable outcomes, supported by a MERL framework, and key performance indicators.\nIt will map relevant DFAT bilateral and regional investments, as well as key development partner investments, and outline how the investment will build on and complement these initiatives. The design will incorporate analysis of GEDSI considerations, approaches to localisation and sustainability, including opportunities to strengthen Cambodian ownership, institutions and systems. This analysis should correlate with choices made in the program logic and identify appropriate entry points for Australia?s investment and RGC commitment.\nThe IDD will confirm stakeholders that should be involved in program implementation and governance and specify suitable governance arrangements, including the roles and responsibilities of partners and practical coordination mechanism between participating schools, Teacher Education Colleges, Provincial Education Offices and other stakeholders.\nIt will include a full risk assessment using DFAT risk tools and templates and develop a risk management strategy, addressing safeguards and compliance with PSEAH, Child Protection, displacement and environmental protection requirements in accordance with Australian and local law.\nThe IDD will also include a program budget suitable for a future tender process and propose clauses for inclusion in an investment-specific Memorandum-of-Understanding (MoU) with the partner government. A draft Scope of Services as an annex. \nThe IDD should be designed with sufficient flexibility to adapt over the ten-year investment period in response to changes in context and evolving Australian and RGC priorities. The Design Team should consider an approach that begins with a targeted intervention and provides scope for expansion based on demonstrated performance and results.\nThe Design Team will also investigate opportunities for innovation in approaches and partnerships that contribute to sustainable improvements in student learning outcomes. This may include innovations in teacher professional development systems, structured pedagogy, digital learning tools, inclusive teaching resources, and locally led development approaches, including partnerships with Australian organisations and universities.\nRefer to Terms of Reference for further information. \n[1] For example, INOVASI?s experience with the Indonesia?Australia Teacher Education Cooperation (IATEC) brings together ten Indonesian and five Australian universities. This initiative aims to support the transformation of teacher education in Indonesia by enhancing the quality of teacher professional education and strengthening the capacity of participating Indonesian universities to deliver effective teacher education programs.\nA.A.2(a)\tCommonwealth Supplier Code of Conduct\nThe Commonwealth expects its suppliers to conduct themselves with high standards of ethics such that they consistently act with integrity and accountability.\xa0\xa0\nIf awarded a Contract, the Supplier must agree to comply with the Commonwealth Code of Conduct (Code) in accordance with the Commonwealth Contract Terms [Clause C.C.23].\xa0\nClause C.C.23 requires the Supplier to comply with the Code when performing its obligations under the Contract, and to ensure its personnel and Subcontractors comply with the Code. The clause also requires the Supplier to proactively monitor and assess compliance with the Code, and to notify the Customer immediately of any breach of the Code.\xa0\xa0\nIn accordance with the Commonwealth ATM Terms [Clause A.B.10], where requested by the Customer, Potential Suppliers should provide information demonstrating that they have appropriate policies, frameworks, or similar, in place to comply with the Code.\xa0\xa0\nFurther information on the Code can be found at: https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-supplier-code-conduct \nA.A.2(b)\tStandards\nPotential Suppliers must ensure that any goods and services proposed are capable of complying with all applicable Australian standards and any Australian and international standards specified in this Statement of Requirement. Potential Suppliers should note that they may be required to enable the Customer, or an independent assessor, to conduct periodic audits to confirm compliance with those standards.\nWeb Content Accessibility\nThe Supplier must ensure that any website, associated material and/or online publications (where applicable) complies with the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines available at: https://www.w3.org/WAI/intro/wcag.\nA.A.2(c)\tSecurity Requirements\nA.A.2(d)\tWork Health and Safety\nPrior to commencement of the Contract, the Customer?s Contract Manager and the Supplier?s Contract Manager will identify any potential work health and safety (WHS) issues anticipated to arise during the term of the contract and assign management of each issue identified to the party best able to manage it. For all issues assigned to the Supplier, the\xa0Supplier will provide the Customer with a WHS plan for approval and no work will commence until the plan is approved unless agreed in writing by the Customer.\nThroughout the Contract Term, the Customer and the Supplier will proactively identify and cooperate to manage any WHS issues that arise.\nA.A.2(e)\tDelivery and Acceptance\nThe Customer must accept or reject any deliverables under the Contract in accordance with the Commonwealth Contract Terms [Clause C.C.11].\nA.A.2(f)\tMeetings\nThe Supplier will be required to attend meetings as follows:\nA.A.2(g)\tFacilities and Assistance Offered by the Customer\nThe Customer will not make any facilities or assistance available to the Supplier.\nA.A.2(h)\tCustomer Material\nDFAT Design, Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning Standards (latest version Feb 2026). \nAustralia?s International Development Policy, Australia-Cambodia Development Partnership Plan (2025-2029); International Gender Equality Strategy and International Disability Equity and Rights Strategy (2024); DFAT Guidance Note: Locally Led Development and Invested: Australia?s Southeast Asia Economic Strategy.  \nAustralia-Cambodia Development Partnership Plan (DPP) 2025-2029\nRGC policies: Royal Government of Cambodia Pentagonal Strategy - Phase 1; MoEYS Education Strategic Plan 2024-2028; Teacher Policy Action Plan 2024-2030; Master Plan on Gender and Climate Change 2018-2030\nAnalysis from the What Works Hub for Global Education (if available) \nWorld Bank and GPE evaluation of Cambodia?s education sector.\nOther partner and DFAT program documentation.\nA.A.3\tATM Distribution\nAusTender Distribution\nThis ATM and any updates are subject to the AusTender\xa0Terms\xa0of\xa0Use. Any updates to this ATM will be distributed via AusTender. \nAusTender is the Australian Government?s procurement information system. Access to and use of AusTender is subject to the AusTender Terms of Use. In participating in this ATM process, Potential Suppliers must comply with the AusTender Terms of Use and any applicable instructions, processes, procedures and recommendations as advised on AusTender.\nA.A.4\tLodgement of Responses\nAusTender\nResponses must be lodged electronically via AusTender before the Closing Time and in accordance with the Response lodgement procedures set out in this ATM documentation and on AusTender.\nAll queries for technical or operational support must be directed to the AusTender Help Desk. Contact details are available on the Contact Us page of the AusTender website.\nThe Closing Time will be displayed on the relevant AusTender webpage together with a countdown clock that displays, in real time, the time left until Closing Time (for more information refer to AusTender Terms of Use).\nFor the purpose of determining whether a Response has been lodged before the Closing Time, the countdown clock will be conclusive.\nResponse File Format, Naming Convention and Size\nThe Customer will accept Responses lodged in the following formats:\nMicrosoft Word (.docx)\nPDF (.pdf)\nCSV (.csv)\nThe Response file name/s should:\nincorporate the Potential Supplier?s full legal organisation name; and \nreflect the various parts of the bid they represent (where the Response comprises multiple files).\nResponse files must not exceed a combined file size of  megabytes per email.\nResponses must be completely self-contained. No embedded files can be included. No hyperlinked or other material may be incorporated by reference.\nA.A.5\tCustomer?s Contact Officers\nA.A.5(a)\tATM Contact Officer\nFor all matters relating to this ATM, the Contact Officer is:\nName/Position:\tTender Secretariat\nEmail Address:\tTender.Cambodia@dfat.gov.au\nNote: Question Closing Date and Time is set out at item A.A.1 [Key Information and Dates].\nA.A.5(b)\tComplaints Handling\nComplaints relating to this ATM should be directed to:\nInformation relating to the handling of complaints is available on the Customer?s website at: https://www.dfat.gov.au/about-us/publications/complaints-handling-procedures-procurement.\nIf your issue is not resolved refer https://www.finance.gov.au/business/procurement/complaints-handling-charter-complaints for more information relating to complaints.\nAdditional Contract Terms\nAn executed contract will incorporate the Commonwealth Contract Terms and also the following Additional Contract Terms:\nA.C.1\tIntellectual Property\nThe Customer owns the Intellectual Property Rights in the Material created under the Contract. \nTo the extent the Supplier or a third party holds any Intellectual Property Rights in any existing Material, the Supplier hereby agrees to licence the Customer to enable the Customer to exercise full rights and interests in the Intellectual Property Rights in any Material provided under the Contract. The Supplier agrees to create, execute or sign any documents and perform all acts which may be necessary to allow the use of those rights by the Customer for any purpose.\nThe Customer grants to the Supplier a non-exclusive, non-transferable, irrevocable, royalty-free licence for the Contract Term to exercise the Intellectual Property Rights in the Material for the sole purpose of fulfilling its obligations under the Contract. The licence in this clause is subject to any conditions or limitations of third parties that the Customer notifies to the Supplier.\nIntellectual Property Rights in Goods provided under the Contract or pre-existing Intellectual Property of the Supplier, set out below (if any), will not change as a result of the Contract.\nA.C.2\tPayment Terms\nThe Customer must pay the amount of a Correctly Rendered Invoice to the Supplier within twenty\xa0(20)\xa0calendar days after receiving it, or if this day is not a Business Day, on the next Business Day.\nA.C.3\tFraud\nA.C.3.1\tThis clause replaces clause C.C.22.F [Fraud] of the Commonwealth Contract Terms. For the purposes of this clause, ?Fraud? means, in delivering the Goods and/or Services under the Contract, dishonestly obtaining a benefit, or causing a loss, by deception or other means, and includes suspected, alleged or attempted fraud.\nA.C.3.2\tThe Supplier must take all reasonable steps to prevent and detect Fraud in relation to the performance of this Contract. \nA.C.3.3\tSubject to A.C.3.4, if the Supplier becomes aware of any Fraud, it must report the matter to the Customer in writing within five (5) business days.\nA.C.3.4\tIf the Supplier is under a legal obligation not to report a Fraud to the Customer, but local police or other law enforcement agencies provide an exception to permit reporting, the Supplier must report a Fraud to the Customer within five (5) business days of the exception being granted.\nA.C.3.5\tThe Supplier must investigate all Fraud at the Supplier?s expense and in accordance with any reasonable directions or standards required by the Customer. After the investigation is finished, the Supplier must promptly report full details of any Fraud to:\nthe Customer (unless the Supplier is under a legal obligation not to report a Fraud to the Customer); and\nthe local police and any other appropriate law enforcement agency in the country where the incident occurred, unless the Customer agrees otherwise in writing. \nA.C.3.6\tIf the investigation finds Fraud by the Supplier or its officers, employees or agents, or the Supplier has failed to take reasonable steps to prevent Fraud by a subcontractor, the Supplier must, if directed by the Customer, promptly reimburse or compensate the Customer in full.\nA.C.3.7\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.4\tProhibited dealings\nA.C.4.1\tThe Supplier must ensure that it and its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors involved in delivering Goods and or Services under this Contract are not:\ndirectly or indirectly engaged in preparing, planning, assisting or fostering a terrorist act;\nlisted terrorist organisations for the purposes of the\xa0Criminal Code Act 1995\xa0(Cth) (details of listed terrorist organisations are available at:\nhttps://www.nationalsecurity.gov.au/Listedterroristorganisations/Pages/default.aspx);\nsubject to sanctions or similar measures under the\xa0Charter of the United Nations Act 1945\xa0(Cth) or the\xa0Autonomous Sanctions Act 2011\xa0(Cth) (details of individuals and entities are available at:\nhttps://dfat.gov.au/international-relations/security/sanctions/Pages/consolidated-list.aspx;\nlisted on the ?World Bank?s Listing of Ineligible Firms and Individuals? posted at:\xa0http://www.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement/debarred-firms;\nowned, controlled by, acting on behalf of, or at the direction of persons, or entities referred to in clauses A.C.4.1(a) to A.C.4.1(d) above; or\nproviding direct or indirect support, resources or assets (including any grant monies) to persons or entities referred to in clauses A.C.4.1(a) to A.C.4.1(e) above.\nA.C.4.2\tWhere the Supplier becomes aware that there are reasonable grounds to suspect it or any of its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors has or may have contravened any part of clause A.C.4, the Supplier must:\nnotify the Customer and confirm that information in writing as soon as possible, which must be no later than within 24 hours;\nimmediately take all reasonable action to mitigate the risks; and\ntake any other action reasonably required by the Customer.\nA.C.4.3\tThe Supplier must ensure that any subcontract entered into by the Supplier for the purposes of fulfilling its obligations under this Contract imposes on the Subcontractor the same obligations that the Supplier has under this clause\xa0A.C.4.\nA.C.4.4\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.5\tAnti-corruption\nA.C.5.1\tThe Supplier warrants that the Supplier, its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors have not made or caused to be made, or received or sought to receive, any offer, gift or payment, consideration or benefit of any kind, which would or could be construed as an illegal or corrupt practice, either directly or indirectly to any party, as an inducement or reward in relation to the execution or performance of the Contract.\nA.C.5.2\tThe Supplier must not, and must ensure that its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors, do not:\nmake or cause to be made, or receive or seek to receive, any offer, gift or payment, consideration or benefit of any kind, which would or could be construed as an illegal or corrupt practice, either directly or indirectly to any party, as an inducement or reward in relation to the performance of the Contract; and/or\nengage in any practice that could constitute the Australian offence of bribing a foreign public official\xa0in relation to the performance of the Contract.\nA.C.5.3\tIf the Supplier becomes aware of any practice, as described in A.C.5.2 (a) or (b), then it must report the matter to the Customer within five (5) business days.\nA.C.5.4\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.6\tChild Protection\nA.C.6.1\tThe Supplier must comply, and must ensure that its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors comply with the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade?s Child Protection Policy, accessible at http://www.dfat.gov.au/childprotection/.\nA.C.6.2\tThe Customer may conduct a review of the Supplier?s compliance with the Child Protection Policy. The Customer will give reasonable notice to the Supplier and the Supplier must participate co-operatively in any such review.\nA.C.6.3\tIf the Customer finds that the Supplier has failed to comply with the Child Protection Policy, the Supplier must promptly, and at the cost of the Supplier, take such actions as are required to ensure compliance with the Child Protection Policy.\nA.C.6.4\tIf an individual, the Supplier must sign and return to the Customer the Child Protection Professional Behaviours at Attachment B to the Child Protection Policy.\nA.C.6.5\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.7\tPreventing Sexual Exploitation Abuse and Harassment \nA.C.7.1\tThe Supplier must comply, and must ensure that its  officers, employees, agents and subcontractors comply with the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade?s Preventing Sexual Exploitation Abuse and Harassment Policy, accessible at https://dfat.gov.au/international-relations/themes/preventing-sexual-exploitation-abuse-and-harassment/Pages/default.aspx \nA.C.7.2\tThe Customer may conduct a review of the Supplier?s compliance with the Preventing Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment Policy. The Customer will give reasonable notice to the Supplier and the Supplier must participate co-operatively in any such review.\nA.C.7.3\tIf the Customer finds that the Supplier has failed to comply with the Preventing Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment Policy, the Supplier must promptly, and at the cost of the Supplier, take such actions as are required to ensure compliance with the Preventing Sexual Exploitation Abuse and Harassment Policy.\nA.C.7.4\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR POTENTIAL SUPPLIER\nBefore you start\nThis Response is not an offer for work.\nRead the all documentation provided by the Customer and distributed with this Response form and decide whether your organisation has the necessary skills and experience to meet the Customer?s requirement.\nDo not proceed further if:\nyour organisation cannot agree to the Commonwealth Contract Terms, available at https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-contracting-suite-ccs. These terms are non-negotiable and will form part of the Contract if you are successful in this ATM process.\nyour organisation does not meet and/or agree to the Minimum Content and Format Requirements and the Conditions for Participation (if any), set out at item A.A.1 [Key Information and Dates], as failure to do so will mean your response cannot be considered.\nyour organisation is a trust where the Trustee is not empowered to sign contracts on behalf of the Trust. Before proceeding, contact the Customer?s ATM Contact Officer at item\xa0A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer].\nyou are an Individual without an ABN and you do not meet the Australian Taxation Office?s (ATO) definition of an independent contractor available at: https://www.ato.gov.au/business/employee-or-contractor/how-to-work-it-out--employee-or-contractor. Before proceeding, contact the ATM Contact Officer set out at item\xa0A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer] to seek advice.\nThe Customer will evaluate all valid Responses received by the ATM Closing Time [Item A.A.1] that meet the Minimum Content and Format Requirements and the Conditions for Participation (if any), to determine which Potential Supplier has proposed the best value for money outcome for the Customer. Responses will be evaluated as per the criteria set out at Clause A.B.5 [Evaluation].\nParticipation in this ATM is at your organisation?s risk and cost. Please note this is a competitive process and your organisation may incur costs in responding. If you are unsuccessful, you will be unable to recoup these costs.\nFormat Requirements\nYou MUST use this form (CCS ATM Response Form) to submit your Response, which MUST comply with the Commonwealth ATM Terms, available at: https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-contracting-suite-ccs.\nAs this form is set out to facilitate the evaluation of responses, Potential Suppliers are to use the form as provided and are not permitted to make any changes to the structure or formatting of the document.\nGuidance for completing your Response\nSpecific questions about this ATM must be directed to the ATM Contact Officer set out at Item\xa0A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer] before Question Closing Date and Time set out at item A.A.1 [Key Information and Dates]. \nIn preparation of this Response, please note the Commonwealth Indigenous Procurement Policy (IPP) available at: https://www.niaa.gov.au/indigenous-affairs/economic-development/indigenous-procurement-policy-ipp may apply to the Customer in respect of this procurement. During evaluation of responses, the Customer may consider the Supplier?s ability to assist the Customer to meet its IPP obligations.\nEnsure your Response is as concise as possible while including all information that your organisation wants the evaluation team to consider. Do not include general marketing material or assume that the evaluation team has any knowledge of your organisation?s capabilities or personnel.\nDo not include pricing in any other part of your response except in the relevant Pricing Schedule.\nThe successful Supplier will have demonstrated its ability to provide the best value for the Customer. This will not necessarily be the lowest price.\nSubmit the form as required by Item A.A.4 [Lodgement of Responses].\nIf your organisation is unsuccessful with this Response, you may request a debrief to assist with future responses. The ATM Contact Officer set out at Item A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer] can arrange this for you.\nPotential Supplier?s Contact Officer\nFor all matters relating to this Response, the Potential Supplier?s Contact Officer is:\nPart 1 ? Potential Supplier?s Details\n1.1\tPotential Supplier?s Details\n1.2\tPrevious Judicial Decisions\n1.3\tWorkplace Gender Equality\n1.4\tIndigenous Businesses\n1.5\tAustralian Businesses\n1.6\tNew Zealand Businesses\n1.7\tPotential Supplier?s Contract Manager\nFor matters relating to the proposed Contract, the Potential Supplier?s Contract Manager will be:\n1.8\tPotential Supplier?s Address for Notices (if different from the Contract Manager)\nPart 2 ? Executive Summary\n2.1\tExecutive Summary of Potential Supplier?s Proposal\n2.2\tConditions for Participation\nPart 3 ? Ability to Meet the Requirement\n3.1\tDetailed Proposal to Meet the Customer?s Requirement\nCommonwealth Supplier Code of Conduct\nStandards\nPart 4 ? Potential Supplier?s Demonstrated Capability and Capacity\n4.1\tStatement of Skills and Experience\n4.2\tSpecified Personnel\n# if requested at A.A.2(b)\n4.3\tSubcontractors\nScope of Works to be Subcontracted\n4.4\tConflicts of Interest\nThe Potential Supplier has identified the following potential Conflicts of Interest and management strategies:\n4.5\tReferees\n4.6\tPre-existing Intellectual Property of Potential Supplier\n4.7\tConfidentiality of Potential Supplier?s Information\nThe Potential Supplier has assessed that the commercial information in the table below meets the requirements available at https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/buying-australian-government/additional-reporting-confidentiality and is seeking that information be kept confidential.\n4.8\tProven Ability to Meet Regulatory Considerations\n4.9\tSustainability Considerations\n4.10\tAdditional Information\nPart 5 ? Total Costs to be incurred by the Customer\n5.1\tPricing\nFixed Price (including all expenses)\nAdjustment to Fixed Pricing for Contract Variation/Extension\n5.2\tProposed Payment Schedule\n5.3\tAdditional Facilities and Assistance\n5.4\tNon-Compliance\n"}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1112 - Term of Reference - InvestIn Education for Cambodia Design.txt', 'extracted_text': 'AUGUST 2026 \n@DFAT DFAT.GOV.AU \nOFFICIAL \nTERMS OF REFERENCE: \nDESIGN FOR INVESTING IN EDUCATION PROGRAM IN CAMBODIA \n1. INTRODUCTION \nDFAT proposes to deliver an education investment (working title to be confirmed during design) valued at up to \nAUD 50 million over ten years (July 2027 to June 2037) in Cambodia. The investment will support Cambodia?s efforts \nto ensure inclusive and equitable quality education and promote lifelong learning opportunities for all. It will \nstrengthen teaching and learning, inclusion, retention, and education system capacity to improve secondary \neducation outcomes. Over time, it is expected to contribute to improved completion rates and learning outcomes \nat the secondary level. \nCambodia aims to transition to upper-middle-income status by 2030 and high-income status by 2050. To achieve \nthis, the Royal Government of Cambodia (RGC) is increasingly focused on improving the quality of its human capital, \nrecognising that education is central to achieving its Pentagonal Strategy Phase I (2023-2028) and long-term Vision \n2050. By delivering quality secondary education, Cambodia can prepare students for post-secondary education and \nskilled employment in key areas including technology, health, infrastructure, and governance.  \nCambodia?s education system continues to face challenges and opportunities. While access to schooling has \nexpanded significantly over the past two decades, learning outcomes remain a key development challenge, \nparticularly at the lower secondary level (Grades 7-9). This stage is a major bottleneck in the system, with high \ndropout rates, poor transition to upper secondary, and persistent inequities affecting disadvantaged groups.  \nLearning outcomes are a major concern. In 2023, only 12 per cent of 15-year-olds reached minimum proficiency in \nmathematics and 8 per cent in reading. Gaps in foundational skills from primary school continue to affect student \nperformance in secondary education, requiring remedial support and differentiated teaching. Teacher training \ncolleges report shortages of qualified subject-specialist teachers, particularly in mathematics and science, and many \nteachers lack digital competencies. Without stronger teacher capacity and inclusive pedagogy, students struggle to \nprogress and complete secondary education. \nCambodia continues to face challenges in ensuring an adequate supply of qualified teachers. Despite significant pay \nrises in the past decade, teacher salaries remain modest. As a result, many teachers undertake additional work to \nsupplement their income, including private tutoring outside school hours. Some stakeholders have raised concerns \nabout the potential implications of these practices for equitable access to learning opportunities. These practices \nmay have implications for educational equity and access for lower-income households, reduce time available for \nnon-teaching duties such as lesson planning and student feedback, and contribute to teacher workload pressures. \nAs Cambodia?s economy has grown, there has been increased participation by non-government education \nproviders, with more middle class families choosing private schools, particularly in Phnom Penh. As more \nadvantaged students leave the public system, public schools are left with a higher concentration of lower-income \nstudents and those with greater educational needs, reducing the positive peer effects that can support academic \nachievement.  \nThe design will assess the most effective approaches for improving learning outcomes for lower secondary students. \nPriority areas for further investigation include teacher workforce development systems; classroom level teaching \nand learning inputs and curriculum reform.   \nParticular attention should also be given to understanding the transferability of promising approaches already being \nimplemented at primary level, including differentiated learning, structured pedagogy, teacher coaching and \ncontinuous professional development models, to the lower secondary context. \nGiven the available resources, the design should assess where Australian support can make the greatest \ncontribution to  learning outcomes and retention within the lower secondary sub-sector and recommend a focused \nset of reform priorities supported by evidence and stakeholder consultation. \n\n \n2 \n \nAustralia has previously supported Cambodia?s education sector through Australia Awards Cambodia scholarships, \ncontributions to the Global Partnership for Education (GPE), and regional initiatives such as UNICEF?s Empowered \nand Equal Futures for Girls in Southeast Asia. However, this will be Australia?s first dedicated bilateral education \ninvestment in Cambodia, making it strategically important for strengthening Australia?s partnership with \nCambodia?s education sector and key stakeholders. \nBy focusing on lower secondary education, the investment will complement existing development partner programs \nthat primarily target early childhood and primary education, while strengthening Australia?s engagement and \ncooperation in an important sub-sector.  \nThe proposed investment is not intended to duplicate existing investments by the World Bank, GPE, UNICEF or JICA. \nInstead, it will focus on the comparatively underserved lower secondary sub-sector, where Australia can add value \nand complement partner efforts. The design will examine global ?smart buys?1 and lessons learned from other \neducation investments in Cambodia, including the importance of structured pedagogy, sustainable continuous \nprofessional development (CPD) systems and inclusive teaching practices. \nThe investment will strengthen Gender Equality, Disability Inclusion, and Social Inclusion (GEDSI) by promoting \ninclusive teaching practices, supporting vulnerable students, and addressing boys? underperformance relative to \ngirls.  \nThe investment will also have a strong localisation focus, including exploration of different delivery models and \nengagement of diverse local actors in the design. GEDSI will be central to the investment?s theory of change.  \nThe IDD will identify practical approaches to localisation, including opportunities to strengthen Cambodian \nownership, systems and institutions, promote locally led development, and support sustainability beyond the period \nof Australian support. \nThe investment will additionally contribute to Cambodia?s longer term resilience including in the context of climate \nchange. The design process will explore opportunities to strengthen climate resilience across the education system, \nincluding through greater emphasis on STEM education to build the skills and competencies needed to understand \nand respond to climate-related challenges including through the clean energy transition and climate resilience. \nClimate Change will be considered as a secondary objective during the first 5-year phase of the investment. \nDFAT wishes to procure a design for the new education investment that will support Cambodia to translate its \ndemographic dividend into higher productivity, stronger human capital and sustained economic growth. The \ninvestment will build on established relationships through Australia Awards Cambodia, support through the GPE, \nand broader regional cooperation, drawing on lessons learned from other DFAT and partner programs.  \n2. OBJECTIVES \nThe Design Team will produce an Investment Design Document (IDD) for the proposed InvestIn Education for \nCambodia Program for an indicative timeframe of up to ten years (July 2027 to June 2037), with an initial five-year \nphase (2027-2032) and a budget of up to AUD 50 million. The design will assess and recommend appropriate \ndelivery and implementation arrangements, including consideration of value-for-money, feasibility and sustainably. \nIt will consider implementation approaches that enable future planning, continuation and/or expansion of the \ninvestment beyond 2032, subject to mid-term review and performance, and accommodate different funding \nscenarios above and below the indicative AUD 50 million allocation to ensure flexibility and scalability. \nThe IDD will reflect a consultative design process that tests, confirms, refines and, where appropriate, revises the \nindicative End of Program Outcomes (EOPO) and Intermediate Outcomes (IOs) outlined in the Investment Concept \nNote (ICN). The Design Team may recommend alternative outcomes structures where supported by evidence, \nstakeholder consultation and analysis. \nThe design will reflect and align with the priorities of the RGC, including the Education Strategic Plan 2024-2030 and \nVision 2050, and be consistent with Ministry of Education, Youth and Sport (MoEYS) standards for teacher \nperformance, curriculum reform and inclusive education. It will also reflect and align with the directions and \n \n1 Global Education Evidence Advisory Panel (GEEAP). (2023). Cost-Effective Approaches to Improve Global Learning: What Does Recent Evidence Tell Us Are "Smart Buys" for Improving Learning in Low- \nand Middle-Income Countries? London, Washington, DC, New York: Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO), World Bank, UNICEF and USAID. \n\n \n3 \n \npriorities of the Australia-Cambodia Development Partnership Plan (DPP), ensuring coherence with Australia?s \nbroader development cooperation objectives in Cambodia. The IDD will include a robust program logic, measurable \noutcomes, and gender-, disability- and inclusion-sensitive indicators. \nThe indicative EOPOs and IO?s outlined in the ICN are:  \nEOPO 1: Teachers in targeted lower secondary schools deliver more effective, inclusive, and evidence-based \nteaching that improves student engagement and learning outcomes. \nIntermediate Outcomes:  \n? \nTeachers at targeted lower secondary schools demonstrate more inclusive and evidence-based teaching \npractices, including the effective use of formative assessment and differentiated instruction. \n? \nTargeted Teacher Education Colleges provide sustainable systems of continuous professional \ndevelopment that strengthen teaching quality across lower secondary schools in their region. \n? \nEducation authorities use data and evidence to continuously improve teacher professional development \nand instructional support. \nEOPO 2: Targeted lower secondary schools provide safer, more inclusive, and resilient learning environments that \nsupport equitable participation and continuity of learning. \nIntermediate Outcomes: \n? \nSchool leaders and teachers implement gender-responsive pedagogies and positive behaviour \napproaches that promote equitable participation and foster safe, respectful learning environments. \n? \nTargeted schools strengthen inclusive education practices and provide appropriate support for students \nwith disability and other learners at risk of exclusion. \n? \nTargeted schools and education institutions strengthen disaster preparedness and climate resilience to \nmaintain safe and continuous learning during climate- and disaster-related disruptions \n3. SCOPE \nThe design will examine the challenges, opportunities and constraints affecting learning outcomes, participation \nand completion at the lower secondary level in Cambodia. Drawing on stakeholder consultations, analytical work, \ninternational evidence, and lesson from existing investments, the design will assess how Australia can most \neffectively support improved teaching and learning outcomes within the lower secondary sub-sector, while aligning \nwith Cambodia?s education reform priorities and Australia?s broader development objectives.  \nThe design will assess the relevance and feasibility of different intervention pathways, including teacher workforce \ndevelopment systems, continuous professional development, curriculum and assessment reform, teaching and \nlearning materials and inclusive education approaches and institutional strengthening at national and sub-national \nlevels. Particular attention should be given to understanding the factors that constrain learning outcomes, \nparticipation and transition through lower secondary education, especially for children with disabilities, Indigenous \nlearners, rural students and boys at risk of underperformance.  \nThe design will examine the institutional, policy and operating environment within which the investment will be \nimplemented, including MoEYS, Provincial and District Offices of Education, Teacher Education Colleges, schools, \ndevelopment partners and other stakeholders. The design should address opportunities to strengthen the \nimplemention of national reforms at sub-national level and school levels and identify the most effective balance \nbetween national, sub-national and school level engagement. The design will also consider recent policy and \ninstitutional developments, including the Education Strategic Plan 2024-2028, Teacher Policy Action Plan 2024-\n2030, Education 2040 Roadmap, Pentagonal Strategy Phase 1, Vision 2050 and relevant climate change inclusion \npolicies. The design should assess how the proposed investments and contribute to Cambodia?s human capital \ndevelopment objectives and complement existing development partner investments. This should include \nconsideration of how evidence-based approaches currently being implemented at primary level, including \n\n \n4 \n \ndifferentiated learning, structured pedagogy and teacher professional development models, may be adapted or \nextended to the lower secondary context. \nThe design will examine a range of delivery models and implementation arrangements, including multilateral \npartnerships, grant mechanisms NGO partnerships, research partnerships, managing contractor approaches, and \ncombinations thereof. The analysis should assess the relative advantages and disadvantages of each option, \nincluding feasibility, value-for-money, scalability, sustainability, risk, localisation, implementation complexity and \nalignment with Australia?s comparative advantage. The Design Team should recommend a preferred approach and \nprovide a clear rationale for that recommendation. \nThe design will identify opportunities to strengthen GEDSI throughout the investment. This should include \nconsideration of barriers faced by children with disability, girls, boys, Indigenous learners and others marginalised \ngroups. The design should examine the factors affecting enrolment, retention, participation and learning outcomes, \nincluding any gendered drivers of dropout and barriers affecting children with disabilities. \nThe design will also assess climate change and disaster risks relevant to the education sector and identify \nopportunities to strengthen resilience and continuity of learning. Particular attention should be given to local and \npractical and cost-effective approach that can be integrated into the investment and sustained by Cambodia? \ninstitutions over time. \nDrawing on consultations, evidence and analysis the Design Team will recommend the most effective approach for \nAustralia?s engagement in the lower secondary sub-sector. This should include recommendations on reform \npriorities, geographic focus, implementation arrangements, partnership models, governance arrangements, \ndelivery modality and opportunities for phased expansion of the investment over time. Recommendations should \nbe supported by evidence, stakeholder consultation and value-for-money considerations.  \n3.1.1. Key Design Questions \nIn undertaking the design, the Design Team should address the following key design questions: \n? \nWhat is the optimal balance between national, sub-national and school-level support required to improve \nlearning outcomes and completion rates? \n? \nWhich intervention pathways offer the strongest evidence of effectiveness and value for money? \n? \nTo what extent can differentiated learning, structured pedagogy and teacher professional development \napproaches be adapted effectively to lower secondary education? \n? \nWhat factors influence dropout, retention and participation in lower secondary education, including \ngender-specific drivers? \n? \nWhat barriers prevent children with disabilities from enrolling, participating and succeeding in lower \nsecondary education? \n? \nWhich delivery modality provides the best balance of effectiveness, sustainability, localisation and \nengagement? \nThe Design Team may propose additional key design questions through the Design Plan. \n4. DELIVERABLES \nThe Design Team will produce an IDD that meets DFAT?s Investment Design Quality Criteria and Design, Monitoring, \nEvaluation and Learning (DMEL) Standards. The IDD will include a robust program logic, Monitoring, Evaluation, \nResearch and Learning (MERL) framework, risk assessment and management strategy, governance arrangements, \nbudget, and an implementation approach suitable for procurement and implementation. \nThe Design Team will develop a Design Plan, in consultation with DFAT, that meets DFAT?s DMEL Standards. The \nDesign Team will be responsible for organising stakeholder consultations, with DFAT providing limited support \nwhere required. \n\n \n5 \n \nThe Design Team will develop an Options Paper (PowerPoint) for DFAT, drawing on information gathered through \nstakeholder consultations and analytical work from the What Works Hub for Global Education (WWGHE). The \nOptions Paper will be discussed and agreed at the conclusion of the in-country mission. It will summarise: \n? \nkey findings from desk-based research and consultations; \n? \nprovide recommendations on the most effective approach for supporting improved teaching and learning \noutcomes within Cambodia\'s lower secondary sub-sector, including reform priorities, geographic focus, \ndelivery pathways and supporting rationale; \n? \noutline options for the national and sub-national allocation of resources; \n? \nprovide recommendations on geographic focus, including specific institutions to be targeted or a \nmethodology for identifying priority locations and institutions;  \n? \nrecommend feasible delivery options and explain why the proposed arrangements represent value-for-\nmoney; and  \n? \nidentify any potential quick wins. \nWithin one week of the completion of the in-country mission, the Design Team will finalise an aide m?moire \n(PowerPoint presentation and document) reflecting the key findings and agreed outcomes from discussions on the \noptions paper. \nThe Design Team will prepare first and second draft versions of the IDD for DFAT review and input, followed by a \nfinal IDD that meets DFAT?s DMEL Standards. The final IDD will include a full description of the rationale for the \ninvestment, including justification for investing in Cambodia?s lower secondary education sub-sector. This will \ninclude problem analysis and identify Australia?s comparative advantage within the available budget envelope.  \nThe IDD will examine the feasibility and viability of available delivery options, including multilateral grant, NGO \npartnership, managing contractor, or a combination thereof, and explain why the proposed arrangements \nrepresent value for money. \nThe IDD will include a program logic with achievable and sustainable outcomes, supported by a MERL framework, \nand key performance indicators. \nIt will map relevant DFAT bilateral and regional investments, as well as key development partner investments, and \noutline how the investment will build on and complement these initiatives. The design will incorporate analysis of \nGEDSI considerations, approaches to localisation and sustainability, including opportunities to strengthen \nCambodian ownership, institutions and systems. This analysis should correlate with choices made in the program \nlogic and identify appropriate entry points for Australia?s investment and RGC commitment. \nThe IDD will confirm stakeholders that should be involved in program implementation and governance and specify \nsuitable governance arrangements, including the roles and responsibilities of partners and practical coordination \nmechanism between participating schools, Teacher Education Colleges, Provincial Education Offices and other \nstakeholders. \nIt will include a full risk assessment using DFAT risk tools and templates and develop a risk management strategy, \naddressing safeguards and compliance with PSEAH, Child Protection, displacement and environmental protection \nrequirements in accordance with Australian and local law. \nThe IDD will also include a program budget suitable for a future tender process and propose clauses for inclusion in \nan investment-specific Memorandum-of-Understanding (MoU) with the partner government. A draft Scope of \nServices as an annex.  \nThe IDD should be designed with sufficient flexibility to adapt over the ten-year investment period in response to \nchanges in context and evolving Australian and RGC priorities. The Design Team should consider an approach that \nbegins with a targeted intervention and provides scope for expansion based on demonstrated performance and \nresults. \n\n \n6 \n \nThe Design Team will also investigate opportunities for innovation in approaches and partnerships that contribute \nto sustainable improvements in student learning outcomes. This may include innovations in teacher professional \ndevelopment systems, structured pedagogy, digital learning tools, inclusive teaching resources, and locally led \ndevelopment approaches, including partnerships with Australian organisations and universities.2 \n4.1.1. Table 1. Table of Deliverables \nDeliverable \nDescription \nIndicative \nTiming \nDesign Plan \nDesign methodology, workplan, consultation approach, \nstakeholder engagement strategy, roles and responsibilities, key \ndesign questions, analytical framework, and approach to testing \nthe assumptions outlined in the ICN. \nOctober 2026 \nOptions Paper \n(PowerPoint) \nSummary of findings from desk research, consultations and \nWWHGE analysis. Includes recommendations on how Australia \ncan best engage in the lower secondary sub-sector, geographic \nfocus, delivery options, implementation approach, and potential \nearly wins. \nEnd of in-\ncountry mission \n(December \n2026) \nAide Memoire \n(PowerPoint \nPresentation and \nDocument) \nSummary of key findings, agreed decisions, and next steps arising \nfrom consultations and Options Paper discussions. \nWithin 1 week \nof in-country \nmission \nExternal Aide Memoire \nPresentation \nPresentation of key findings and emerging design directions to \nMoEYS and other stakeholders, as agreed with DFAT. \nDecember 2026 \nDraft IDD (Version 1) \nFirst draft IDD, including program logic, EOPOs, intermediate \noutcomes, delivery model analysis, MEL framework, governance \narrangements, risk assessment, and budget. \nDecember 2026 \nDraft IDD (Version 2) \nRevised draft incorporating DFAT comments and quality assurance \nfeedback. \nJanuary 2027 \nDraft Statement of \nRequirements (SoR) \nDraft procurement documentation for future implementation \narrangements (if required). \nJanuary-\nFebruary 2027 \nDraft Risk Register and \nRisk Management \nStrategy \nRisk assessment using DFAT templates, including safeguards, \nPSEAH, child protection, fraud and corruption, climate, \nenvironmental and operational risks. \nJanuary-\nFebruary 2027 \nFinal IDD \nFinal design document that meets DFAT Design and MEL \nStandards and incorporates feedback from appraisal and review \nprocesses. \nMarch 2027 \nFinal Annexes \nProgram logic, MEL framework, implementation plan, governance \narrangements, budget, risk register, stakeholder analysis and \nother annexes required by DFAT. \nMarch 2027 \nDraft Pricing Schedule \nIndicative implementation budget and pricing schedule suitable \nfor future procurement. \nMarch 2027 \nIndicative \nImplementation Plan \nHigh-level implementation/workplan for the first 6-12 months of \nthe investment. \nMarch 2027 \n \n2 For example, INOVASI?s experience with the Indonesia?Australia Teacher Education Cooperation (IATEC) brings together ten Indonesian and five Australian universities. This initiative aims to support \nthe transformation of teacher education in Indonesia by enhancing the quality of teacher professional education and strengthening the capacity of participating Indonesian universities to deliver \neffective teacher education programs. \n\n \n7 \n \nAll design outputs must meet DFAT?s documentary standards, including the use of relevant templates. Outputs must \nbe clear, concise, and tailored to DFAT?s needs. DFAT will assess deliverables against its Investment Design Quality \nCriteria, DMEL Standards, and other relevant policies and procedures. \nAnnex A provides a draft indicative timeline, including DFAT?s responsibilities for seeking and returning feedback. \nThe Design Team will finalise the timeline in a simple design workplan. Dates may be adjusted as required, with any \namendments agreed in writing between DFAT and the Team Leader. \nIf required, DFAT may agree to the Team Leader and/or team members undertaking additional in-country visits to \nsupport further engagement during design finalisation, subject to available time and funding. The final deadline \nremains March 2027, with the exact date to be determined once the Design Team has been procured. \n5. AUDIENCES \nThe primary audience for the design findings and recommendations will be DFAT and relevant Canberra-based \nstakeholders. The design will inform DFAT\'s decisions regarding the scope, objectives, delivery approach, \ngovernance arrangements and implementation of the proposed Education in Cambodia investment. \nThe Ministry of Education, Youth and Sport (MoEYS) will be a key audience and partner throughout the design \nprocess. Findings will help inform discussions on priority reforms, implementation approaches, and opportunities \nfor collaboration at national and sub-national levels, while ensuring alignment with RGC policies, priorities and \nsystems. \nOther Cambodian stakeholders, including Provincial and District Offices of Education, Teacher Education Colleges, \nschools, and relevant government agencies, may use the findings to inform planning, coordination and \nimplementation arrangements related to lower secondary education reform. \nDevelopment partners , and other organisations active in the education sector, may use the findings to identify \nopportunities for coordination, complementarity, and collaboration, and to ensure Australia\'s investment fills \nidentified gaps and builds on existing initiatives. \nCivil society organisations, organisations of persons with disabilities (OPDs), research institutions, and education-\nfocused NGOs may find the design findings useful in informing future partnerships, strengthening inclusive \neducation approaches, and identifying opportunities to contribute to implementation and policy dialogue. \n6. DURATION AND PHASING  \nThe assignment will be undertaken through the following process: \n? \nInitial team briefing by DFAT officials at the Australian Embassy Phnom Penh (hereinafter the Embassy) to \nclarify scope, priorities, expectations, and timelines for the assignment. \n? \nDesk-based review of documents, including the ICN, RGC policies, evaluations and program \ndocumentation from current and previous DFAT and other development partner investments.  \n? \nDevelopment of a Design Plan that meets DFAT?s DMEL standards and includes the proposed approach, \nconsultations (remote and in-country), roles and responsibilities, and key design questions, analytical \nframework, and approach to testing the assumptions outlined in the ICN. \n? \nInitial desk-based stakeholder consultations with relevant DFAT thematic groups (Education, Gender, \nDisability, and Climate Change), DFAT regional stakeholders and other Australian experts.  \n? \nIn-country design mission to conduct consultations with potential stakeholders, including the Embassy, \nrelevant RGC ministries, Provincial Education Offices, Teacher Education Colleges, I/NGOs, CSOs, \norganisations of people with disabilities, research organisations, current DFAT programs, development \npartners, school principals, teachers, students and parent associations. The Embassy will provide the \nDesign Team with details of recommended stakeholders, and the Design Team may suggest additional \nstakeholders for inclusion. Logistics will be organised by the Design Team.  \n? \nPresentation of findings on completion of the in-country mission.  \n\n \n8 \n \nPart 1: Options Paper \n? \nSubmission of an Options Paper for Embassy staff and the Canberra desk that draws on information \ngathered through consultations with partners and stakeholders and analytical work from the WWHGE for \ndiscussion and agreement near the end of the in-country mission. The options paper (to be presented in \nPowerPoint format) will outline the preferred direction for the investment and key design decisions.  \nPart 2: Aide M?moire  \n? \nPrepare an external presentation of an aide m?moire, appropriate for RGC stakeholders and other \nrelevant partners, to support testing of key design concepts ahead of finalisation of the IDD. This \npresentation may be delivered either by the Design Team or DFAT.  \n? \nSubmit the Aide M?moire within one week of the end of the in-country mission, reflecting the key \nfindings and agreed outcomes from the options paper discussion. \nDraft Design Documents \n? \nSubmission of the first draft IDD. The draft IDD (up to 25 pages plus annexes, including a draft SoR) should \ninclude the program logic, EOPOs, IOs, MEL framework, governance arrangements, analysis of delivery \noptions, implementation arrangements, risk assessment and costing. \n? \nDesk-based revisions to IDD incorporating DFAT feedback and quality assurance processes.  \n? \nSubmission of the second draft IDD and annexes for DFAT internal review and appraisal. \nFinal Deliverables \n? \nDelivery of the final IDD including annexes, a draft SOR, and Pricing Schedule, within 10 working days of \nreceipt of DFAT?s feedback and appraisal. The final submission should also include an indicative \nimplementation/work plan for the first 6 to 12 months, including sequencing of activities, stakeholder \nengagement, and reporting requirements.  \n? \nDissemination and knowledge sharing: Subject to further discussion with the Embassy, the Design Team is \nexpected to play an active role in disseminating design findings.  \n? \nTimeframes for all deliverables are dependent upon the provision of timely feedback to the Design Team, \nas specified in design timetable below. \n7. SPECIFICATION OF THE TEAM \nThis section sets out the professional requirements for the Design Team. The proposal must clearly describe each \nteam member?s design responsibilities and include an indicative workplan covering all aspects of the design process. \nThe Design Team must provide its own administrative support officer to arrange meetings and manage in-country \nlogistics, including translation services if required. \nCollectively, the Design Team should demonstrate extensive experience designing education programs for DFAT or \nother bilateral or multilateral donors, as well as prior experience with development programs in Southeast Asia, \npreferably Cambodia. The team should have experience working with partner government stakeholders, \nparticularly MoEYS, Teacher Education Colleges, and Provincial Education Offices in Cambodia or similar contexts in \nthe design and implementation of donor-funded programs. Knowledge of Cambodia?s secondary education sector \nis required, including challenges related to lower secondary completion, teacher professional development, and \ncurriculum reform. \nThe Design Team should also demonstrate sound knowledge of human resources capacity building and localisation \nprinciples, with an emphasis on strengthening local institutions. Expertise in MEL is required, including development \nof program logics and MEL frameworks in line with DFAT?s DMEL Standards, DFAT reporting processes, evaluation, \nand applied social research. Experience with MEL in education programs is preferred. \nThe team should have expertise in GEDSI in education, including gender and disability analysis and the successful \nintegration of GEDSI principles into program designs. Sound knowledge of GEDSI context in Cambodia is required. \n\n \n9 \n \nThe team should also be capable of incorporating disaster preparedness and climate change considerations into \nthe design, including support for school and community disaster mitigation and response strategies. \nInclusion of Cambodian nationals or regional experts on the Design Team is strongly encouraged. Participation by \njunior staff is also encouraged to support local capacity development. \nThe Design Team may need to draw on supplementary expertise beyond the core team, including expertise in \ndisability inclusion, child protection, climate change, and disaster management. \n7.1. DFAT PERSONNEL \nThe main focal point for interactions between the Design Team and DFAT will be the Second Secretary at the \nAustralian Embassy in Phnom Penh who will act as the Design Manager and coordinate DFAT inputs throughout the \ndesign process. \nDFAT?s Education and Skills Advisor, Posts Education and Aid Effectiveness Advisors, and relevant helpdesks and \nspecialists, including Education, Climate Change, Disability and Social Inclusion, will contribute as associate \nmembers of a DFAT reference group. \nConsistent with DFAT?s DMEL Standards, the Design Team will maintain regular communication with DFAT during \nthe development of the Design Plan, the in-country mission, preparation of the aide m?moire, and drafting of the \nIDD. DFAT will provide strategic guidance, technical advice and consolidated feedback on key deliverables, and may \ncontribute to the drafting of specific sections where appropriate. \n8. ANNEX A: MILESTONES \nDescription \nIndicative Deadline \nProject Mobilisation and Inception \nDesign Team mobilised. \nOct-26 \nInitial briefing with DFAT staff in Phnom Penh and Canberra to confirm scope, \nexpectations, key design questions, and timelines. \nOct-26 \nWeekly or fortnightly progress meetings with the Embassy. \nThroughout the \nassignment \nInception and Planning \n  \nComplete desk-based review of key documents, including RGC policies, sector \nstrategies, and relevant evaluations and program documentation from the World \nBank, GPE, UNICEF and JICA. \nOct-26 \nReview global evidence and lessons learned, including analysis from the What \nWorks Hub for Global Education and relevant DFAT education investments in the \nregion. \nOct-26 \nSubmit Design Plan to DFAT for review, including the proposed methodology, \nconsultation approach, roles and responsibilities, workplan, and key design \nquestions. \nLate October 2026 \nDFAT provides feedback on the Design Plan. \nEarly November 2026 \nStakeholder Consultations and Mission Preparation \nConduct initial desk-based consultations with DFAT thematic teams (Education, \nGender, Disability and Climate Change), Office of Southeast Asia, and relevant \neducation experts in Australia and the region. \nNov-26 \nFinalise stakeholder schedule, logistics, and interview questions for the in-country \nmission. \nNov-26 \n\n \n10 \n \nIn-country Design Mission \nConduct inception meetings and stakeholder consultations in Phnom Penh with \nMoEYS, Teacher Education Colleges, Provincial Education Offices, development \npartners, NGOs and other stakeholders. \nEarly December 2026 \nUndertake provincial consultations to assess school-level needs and opportunities. \nEarly December 2026 \nFacilitate Theory of Change workshop(s) and consultations with Teacher Education \nColleges, lower secondary schools, and education officials. \nEarly December 2026 \nPresent an Options Paper (PowerPoint) to DFAT, outlining preliminary findings, key \ndesign considerations, and recommendations for the preferred investment \napproach. \nEnd of in-country \nmission (December \n2026) \nPost-mission and Design Development \nSubmit Aide Memoire (PowerPoint) within seven days of completion of the in-\ncountry mission, reflecting key findings, agreed actions, and DFAT feedback on \nproposed options. \nDec-26 \nPresent draft Aide Memoire to key stakeholders, (remote or in person, as agreed). \nDec-26 \nUndertake additional in-country engagement, if required and timing permits, to \nfurther refine design directions. \nDecember 2026 - \nJanuary 2027 \nDesign Drafting and Review \nSubmit first draft IDD. \nDec-26 \nDFAT review and feedback on first draft IDD. \nDec-26 \nSubmit second draft IDD. \nJan-27 \nIndependent appraisal and DFAT review of second draft IDD. \nFeb-27 \nFinalisation \nSubmit final IDD, including annexes, draft Statement of Requirements (SoR), Pricing \nSchedule, and other supporting documentation, incorporating feedback from DFAT \nreview and appraisal processes. \nMar-27 \n \n \n \n\n \n11 \n \n9. ANNEX B: LIST OF RELEVANT DOCUMENTS \n? \nDFAT Design, Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning Standards (latest version Feb 2026).  \n? \nAustralia?s International Development Policy, Australia-Cambodia Development Partnership Plan \n(2025-2029); International Gender Equality Strategy and International Disability Equity and Rights \nStrategy (2024); DFAT Guidance Note: Locally Led Development and Invested: Australia?s Southeast \nAsia Economic Strategy.   \n? \nAustralia-Cambodia Development Partnership Plan (DPP) 2025-2029 \n? \nRGC policies: Royal Government of Cambodia Pentagonal Strategy - Phase 1; MoEYS Education \nStrategic Plan 2024-2028; Teacher Policy Action Plan 2024-2030; Master Plan on Gender and Climate \nChange 2018-2030 \n? \nAnalysis from the What Works Hub for Global Education (if available)  \n? \nWorld Bank and GPE evaluation of Cambodia?s education sector. \n? \nDFAT and other partners program documentation. \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1112.txt', 'extracted_text': '================================================================================\nAUSTENDER DETAILS: Current ATM View - DFAT-1112\n================================================================================\n\nDetail URL: https://www.tenders.gov.au/Atm/Show/abfde26a-615b-44e6-a1da-7891176dd23d\nScraped At: 2026-09-24 21:00:18\n\nSTRUCTURED ATTRIBUTES:\n----------------------------------------\nATM ID: DFAT-1112\nAgency: Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade - Australian Aid Program\nCategory: 80100000 - Management advisory services\nClose Date & Time: 25-Sep-2026 10:00 am (ACT Local Time) Show close time for other time zones\nPublish Date: 28-Aug-2026\nLocation: ACT, NSW, VIC, SA, WA, QLD, NT, TAS Canberra, Sydney, Melbourne, Adelaide, Perth, Brisbane, Darwin, Hobart\nATM Type: Request for Tender\nMulti Agency Access: No\nPanel Arrangement: No\nMulti-stage: No\nDescription: The design of the Investing in Education Program will assess and recommend appropriate delivery and implementation arrangements for the program, including consideration of value-for-money, feasibility and sustainably. It will consider implementation approaches that enable future planning, continuation and/or expansion of the investment beyond 2032.\nOther Instructions: \nConditions for Participation: Please refer to the attached ATM documents for full Conditions of Participation, and Minimum Content and Format Requirements.\nTimeframe for Delivery: 6 months (October 2026 to March 2027) with an option to extend for 3 months (April 2027 to June 2027)\nEstimated Value (AUD): From to $495,000.00\nAddress for Lodgement: www.tenders.gov.au\nAddenda Available: View Addenda\n\n================================================================================\n'}], 'content_hash': None, 'tags': None, 'first_seen_at': None, 'last_scanned_at': '2026-09-24T21:01:20.684318+00:00', 'updated_at': None, 'raw_extra': '{"tags": ["consulting", "professional services"]}', 'embedding': [0.06111998111009598, -0.01901313289999962, -0.053715791553258896, -0.009180807508528233, 0.016915755346417427, 0.04381335899233818, 0.047765955328941345, 0.0152970552444458, 0.05772567167878151, -0.0016728043556213379, -0.02774715982377529, 0.038308270275592804, 0.05170845985412598, 0.02167028933763504, -0.026011914014816284, -0.04917832836508751, 0.01583578810095787, 0.050732798874378204, -0.07884372025728226, -0.024583877995610237, -0.056113094091415405, -0.053075313568115234, -0.007405547425150871, -0.023972248658537865, 0.016701417043805122, 0.0017820285866037011, 0.